1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,323
Sweetheart, the best Christmas cookie I ever

2
00:00:03,323 --> 00:00:05,665
ate had only three ingredients.

3
00:00:05,845 --> 00:00:08,539
Butter, sugar and flour.

4
00:00:08,719 --> 00:00:09,729
That's all.

5
00:00:09,909 --> 00:00:12,045
It came in a round tin with

6
00:00:12,045 --> 00:00:14,419
a Scottish soldier on the lid,

7
00:00:14,419 --> 00:00:15,052
and Mrs.

8
00:00:15,232 --> 00:00:18,364
MacLeod next door made it every December

9
00:00:18,364 --> 00:00:21,339
from her mother's recipe from Glasgow.

10
00:00:21,519 --> 00:00:24,029
One bite, and it melted on my

11
00:00:24,029 --> 00:00:25,501
tongue like snow.

12
00:00:25,851 --> 00:00:28,795
But when people make shortbread at home,

13
00:00:28,795 --> 00:00:30,930
it comes out hard as a brick,

14
00:00:30,930 --> 00:00:33,507
or so crumbly it falls apart before

15
00:00:33,507 --> 00:00:35,127
it reaches your mouth.

16
00:00:35,307 --> 00:00:37,687
So tonight I'm giving you Mrs.

17
00:00:37,867 --> 00:00:40,228
MacLeod's shortbread, every step,

18
00:00:40,228 --> 00:00:42,732
and the four mistakes that ruin it.

19
00:00:42,912 --> 00:00:44,054
And stay with me,

20
00:00:44,054 --> 00:00:46,609
because mistake number four is the one

21
00:00:46,609 --> 00:00:48,424
that even good bakers make,

22
00:00:48,424 --> 00:00:50,642
and it happens before you turn on

23
00:00:50,642 --> 00:00:51,247
the oven.

24
00:00:51,598 --> 00:00:52,550
Mrs.

25
00:00:52,730 --> 00:00:55,877
MacLeod came to Mansfield from Scotland in

26
00:00:55,877 --> 00:00:59,099
nineteen twenty-three, married to a man who

27
00:00:59,099 --> 00:01:00,973
worked at the steel mill.

28
00:01:01,153 --> 00:01:03,202
She had a voice like a song,

29
00:01:03,202 --> 00:01:05,618
and she called every child on the

30
00:01:05,618 --> 00:01:06,424
street hen.

31
00:01:06,604 --> 00:01:08,214
Come here, hen,

32
00:01:08,214 --> 00:01:09,181
try this.

33
00:01:09,361 --> 00:01:11,234
And every Christmas Eve,

34
00:01:11,234 --> 00:01:13,809
she walked down the street with a

35
00:01:13,809 --> 00:01:14,979
basket of tins,

36
00:01:14,979 --> 00:01:16,540
one for every house,

37
00:01:16,540 --> 00:01:18,881
wrapped in a red plaid ribbon.

38
00:01:19,232 --> 00:01:21,419
In Scotland, she told me,

39
00:01:21,419 --> 00:01:23,868
shortbread was for Hogmanay,

40
00:01:23,868 --> 00:01:25,005
the New Year,

41
00:01:25,005 --> 00:01:26,755
more than Christmas.

42
00:01:26,935 --> 00:01:28,859
You gave it to the first person

43
00:01:28,859 --> 00:01:31,589
who walked through your door after midnight,

44
00:01:31,589 --> 00:01:32,148
for luck.

45
00:01:32,328 --> 00:01:35,253
She kept that tradition her whole life

46
00:01:35,253 --> 00:01:35,869
in Ohio.

47
00:01:36,049 --> 00:01:38,327
If you knocked on her door after

48
00:01:38,327 --> 00:01:39,965
midnight on New Year's,

49
00:01:39,965 --> 00:01:42,101
you went home with shortbread,

50
00:01:42,101 --> 00:01:44,094
whether you liked it or not.

51
00:01:44,445 --> 00:01:46,067
When I was fourteen,

52
00:01:46,067 --> 00:01:48,258
I asked her for the recipe,

53
00:01:48,258 --> 00:01:50,287
and she laughed and said,

54
00:01:50,287 --> 00:01:52,154
hen, there's no recipe.

55
00:01:52,334 --> 00:01:53,664
One part sugar,

56
00:01:53,664 --> 00:01:55,172
two parts butter,

57
00:01:55,172 --> 00:01:56,769
three parts flour.

58
00:01:56,949 --> 00:01:59,665
That's all anybody needs to know.

59
00:01:59,845 --> 00:02:01,297
And she was right.

60
00:02:01,477 --> 00:02:03,311
One, two, three.

61
00:02:03,491 --> 00:02:06,042
It's the easiest recipe in the world

62
00:02:06,042 --> 00:02:06,893
to remember.

63
00:02:07,073 --> 00:02:09,049
And in a minute I'll tell you

64
00:02:09,049 --> 00:02:11,843
the secret ingredient she didn't mention,

65
00:02:11,843 --> 00:02:14,433
because it isn't really an ingredient.

66
00:02:14,784 --> 00:02:16,499
Here's what you need,

67
00:02:16,499 --> 00:02:16,989
honey.

68
00:02:17,169 --> 00:02:19,167
One cup of good butter,

69
00:02:19,167 --> 00:02:20,730
that's two sticks,

70
00:02:20,730 --> 00:02:21,512
softened.

71
00:02:21,692 --> 00:02:23,352
Half a cup of sugar.

72
00:02:23,532 --> 00:02:24,542
Mrs.

73
00:02:24,722 --> 00:02:27,126
MacLeod used plain white sugar,

74
00:02:27,126 --> 00:02:29,143
but I like powdered sugar,

75
00:02:29,143 --> 00:02:32,013
because it makes them melt even more.

76
00:02:32,193 --> 00:02:34,468
Two cups of all-purpose flour.

77
00:02:34,648 --> 00:02:36,814
And a quarter teaspoon of salt,

78
00:02:36,814 --> 00:02:38,700
if your butter is unsalted.

79
00:02:38,880 --> 00:02:40,378
That's the whole list.

80
00:02:40,728 --> 00:02:42,423
Now, the butter.

81
00:02:42,603 --> 00:02:44,743
With only three ingredients,

82
00:02:44,743 --> 00:02:46,654
the butter is everything.

83
00:02:46,834 --> 00:02:49,331
Buy the best butter you can afford.

84
00:02:49,511 --> 00:02:51,523
The European-style butters,

85
00:02:51,523 --> 00:02:53,237
with a little more fat,

86
00:02:53,237 --> 00:02:56,442
make the most tender shortbread you've ever

87
00:02:56,442 --> 00:02:56,964
tasted.

88
00:02:57,144 --> 00:02:58,108
Mrs.

89
00:02:58,288 --> 00:03:01,300
MacLeod said, if you use cheap butter,

90
00:03:01,300 --> 00:03:03,758
you're making cheap shortbread,

91
00:03:03,758 --> 00:03:05,740
and I won't have it in my

92
00:03:05,740 --> 00:03:06,136
tins.

93
00:03:06,487 --> 00:03:07,971
And one more thing,

94
00:03:07,971 --> 00:03:10,550
a trick for a more tender cookie.

95
00:03:10,730 --> 00:03:13,094
Replace a quarter cup of the flour

96
00:03:13,094 --> 00:03:15,389
with a quarter cup of cornstarch,

97
00:03:15,389 --> 00:03:17,406
or rice flour if you can find

98
00:03:17,406 --> 00:03:17,615
it.

99
00:03:17,795 --> 00:03:20,742
It makes the shortbread sandier and more

100
00:03:20,742 --> 00:03:21,405
delicate.

101
00:03:21,585 --> 00:03:22,700
Mrs.

102
00:03:22,880 --> 00:03:24,761
MacLeod used rice flour,

103
00:03:24,761 --> 00:03:27,505
and it was the reason hers crumbled

104
00:03:27,505 --> 00:03:29,465
just right in your mouth,

105
00:03:29,465 --> 00:03:31,111
but not in your hand.

106
00:03:31,462 --> 00:03:32,414
Step one.

107
00:03:32,594 --> 00:03:34,918
Beat the soft butter with the sugar

108
00:03:34,918 --> 00:03:36,843
until it's smooth and creamy,

109
00:03:36,843 --> 00:03:38,039
about two minutes.

110
00:03:38,219 --> 00:03:40,784
Not fluffy, just smooth.

111
00:03:40,964 --> 00:03:43,339
Then add the flour and salt,

112
00:03:43,339 --> 00:03:44,865
and mix it slowly,

113
00:03:44,865 --> 00:03:47,834
only until it comes together into a

114
00:03:47,834 --> 00:03:48,766
soft dough.

115
00:03:48,946 --> 00:03:51,491
Stop the minute you can't see dry

116
00:03:51,491 --> 00:03:51,953
flour.

117
00:03:52,133 --> 00:03:54,165
It will look a little crumbly.

118
00:03:54,345 --> 00:03:56,725
Press it together with your hands.

119
00:03:57,075 --> 00:03:59,248
And this is mistake number one,

120
00:03:59,248 --> 00:04:01,843
the one that makes shortbread hard as

121
00:04:01,843 --> 00:04:02,404
a brick.

122
00:04:02,584 --> 00:04:03,837
Overmixing.

123
00:04:04,017 --> 00:04:05,892
The more you work flour,

124
00:04:05,892 --> 00:04:07,454
the tougher it gets.

125
00:04:07,634 --> 00:04:10,309
Shortbread should be handled as little as

126
00:04:10,309 --> 00:04:10,896
possible.

127
00:04:11,076 --> 00:04:12,156
Mrs.

128
00:04:12,336 --> 00:04:16,016
MacLeod mixed hers with her fingertips in

129
00:04:16,016 --> 00:04:17,542
about one minute,

130
00:04:17,542 --> 00:04:18,708
and she said,

131
00:04:18,708 --> 00:04:20,324
gentle hands, hen.

132
00:04:20,504 --> 00:04:23,244
Gentle hands make tender shortbread.

133
00:04:23,594 --> 00:04:25,191
If this is helping you,

134
00:04:25,191 --> 00:04:27,553
honey, tap the like button for me.

135
00:04:27,733 --> 00:04:30,426
It helps Grandma more than you know.

136
00:04:30,606 --> 00:04:32,301
Now, step two.

137
00:04:32,481 --> 00:04:34,707
Press the dough into an eight or

138
00:04:34,707 --> 00:04:36,446
nine inch round cake pan,

139
00:04:36,446 --> 00:04:37,559
or a square pan,

140
00:04:37,559 --> 00:04:39,646
in an even layer about half an

141
00:04:39,646 --> 00:04:40,411
inch thick.

142
00:04:40,591 --> 00:04:43,058
Smooth the top with the bottom of

143
00:04:43,058 --> 00:04:43,656
a glass.

144
00:04:43,836 --> 00:04:45,940
Then prick it all over with a

145
00:04:45,940 --> 00:04:47,319
fork, in neat rows.

146
00:04:47,669 --> 00:04:48,681
Why the fork,

147
00:04:48,681 --> 00:04:49,538
sweetheart?

148
00:04:49,718 --> 00:04:52,111
So the steam can escape while it

149
00:04:52,111 --> 00:04:55,103
bakes, and the shortbread stays flat and

150
00:04:55,103 --> 00:04:56,823
doesn't puff or bubble.

151
00:04:57,003 --> 00:04:59,696
And it makes that pretty pattern you

152
00:04:59,696 --> 00:05:00,892
see on the tins.

153
00:05:01,072 --> 00:05:02,655
Then, with a knife,

154
00:05:02,655 --> 00:05:05,072
score it lightly into wedges,

155
00:05:05,072 --> 00:05:05,988
like a pie,

156
00:05:05,988 --> 00:05:07,321
or into fingers,

157
00:05:07,321 --> 00:05:09,571
before it goes in the oven.

158
00:05:09,751 --> 00:05:12,780
It will break cleanly along those lines

159
00:05:12,780 --> 00:05:13,246
later.

160
00:05:13,596 --> 00:05:16,920
Step three, and here's the secret ingredient

161
00:05:16,920 --> 00:05:18,054
I promised you.

162
00:05:18,234 --> 00:05:19,546
Time in the cold.

163
00:05:19,726 --> 00:05:21,757
Put the pan in the icebox for

164
00:05:21,757 --> 00:05:23,789
thirty minutes before baking.

165
00:05:23,969 --> 00:05:25,952
Cold butter holds the shape,

166
00:05:25,952 --> 00:05:29,422
and the shortbread bakes evenly without spreading

167
00:05:29,422 --> 00:05:30,343
at the edges.

168
00:05:30,523 --> 00:05:31,429
Mrs.

169
00:05:31,609 --> 00:05:34,453
MacLeod didn't call it an ingredient.

170
00:05:34,633 --> 00:05:35,858
She just said,

171
00:05:35,858 --> 00:05:36,908
let it rest,

172
00:05:36,908 --> 00:05:39,533
the way you rest after supper.

173
00:05:39,883 --> 00:05:40,951
Step four.

174
00:05:41,131 --> 00:05:44,012
Bake at three hundred twenty-five degrees,

175
00:05:44,012 --> 00:05:47,098
low, for about thirty to thirty-five minutes.

176
00:05:47,278 --> 00:05:49,519
And here's mistake number two.

177
00:05:49,699 --> 00:05:50,779
Too hot an oven.

178
00:05:50,959 --> 00:05:52,964
Shortbread should be pale,

179
00:05:52,964 --> 00:05:54,352
the color of sand,

180
00:05:54,352 --> 00:05:56,974
with just the faintest gold at the

181
00:05:56,974 --> 00:05:57,437
edges.

182
00:05:57,617 --> 00:05:58,785
If it browns,

183
00:05:58,785 --> 00:06:01,030
it turns hard and bitter.

184
00:06:01,210 --> 00:06:02,857
Low and slow,

185
00:06:02,857 --> 00:06:03,997
and pale.

186
00:06:04,347 --> 00:06:06,243
How do you know it's done,

187
00:06:06,243 --> 00:06:06,680
honey?

188
00:06:06,860 --> 00:06:08,787
Don't go by the clock alone.

189
00:06:08,967 --> 00:06:10,546
Look at the edges.

190
00:06:10,726 --> 00:06:13,339
They should be just barely golden,

191
00:06:13,339 --> 00:06:14,799
the color of straw,

192
00:06:14,799 --> 00:06:17,181
while the middle is still pale.

193
00:06:17,361 --> 00:06:20,368
Press the center lightly with your finger.

194
00:06:20,548 --> 00:06:22,346
It should feel set,

195
00:06:22,346 --> 00:06:24,333
not soft and squishy.

196
00:06:24,513 --> 00:06:26,220
Every oven is different.

197
00:06:26,400 --> 00:06:27,579
Mine runs hot,

198
00:06:27,579 --> 00:06:30,527
so I check at twenty-eight minutes,

199
00:06:30,527 --> 00:06:32,042
every single time.

200
00:06:32,393 --> 00:06:33,542
Step five.

201
00:06:33,722 --> 00:06:35,444
As soon as it comes out,

202
00:06:35,444 --> 00:06:37,669
while it's still warm and soft,

203
00:06:37,669 --> 00:06:40,397
cut along your scored lines again with

204
00:06:40,397 --> 00:06:41,401
a sharp knife,

205
00:06:41,401 --> 00:06:43,985
and sprinkle it with a little sugar.

206
00:06:44,165 --> 00:06:46,339
Then leave it in the pan to

207
00:06:46,339 --> 00:06:49,076
cool completely, at least an hour.

208
00:06:49,256 --> 00:06:52,160
Mistake number three is taking it out

209
00:06:52,160 --> 00:06:52,867
too soon.

210
00:06:53,047 --> 00:06:55,473
Warm shortbread falls apart.

211
00:06:55,653 --> 00:06:58,289
Cool shortbread snaps clean.

212
00:06:58,639 --> 00:07:00,558
So far, gentle hands,

213
00:07:00,558 --> 00:07:03,391
the fork, the rest in the cold,

214
00:07:03,391 --> 00:07:04,396
a low oven,

215
00:07:04,396 --> 00:07:06,498
and cooling in the pan.

216
00:07:06,678 --> 00:07:08,217
Now the fourth mistake,

217
00:07:08,217 --> 00:07:10,023
the one I told you about at

218
00:07:10,023 --> 00:07:12,565
the start, the one that happens before

219
00:07:12,565 --> 00:07:13,970
you turn on the oven.

220
00:07:14,150 --> 00:07:16,083
Butter that's too soft,

221
00:07:16,083 --> 00:07:16,924
or melted.

222
00:07:17,104 --> 00:07:19,778
If the butter is greasy and shiny,

223
00:07:19,778 --> 00:07:22,925
your shortbread will be greasy and flat,

224
00:07:22,925 --> 00:07:25,127
no matter what you do after.

225
00:07:25,477 --> 00:07:27,881
Soft butter should hold the dent of

226
00:07:27,881 --> 00:07:30,079
your finger, but not be shiny or

227
00:07:30,079 --> 00:07:30,422
oily.

228
00:07:30,602 --> 00:07:32,907
Leave it on the counter for about

229
00:07:32,907 --> 00:07:35,003
an hour, not in the microwave.

230
00:07:35,183 --> 00:07:36,197
If you forgot,

231
00:07:36,197 --> 00:07:38,372
cut it into small cubes and it

232
00:07:38,372 --> 00:07:40,546
will soften in twenty minutes.

233
00:07:40,726 --> 00:07:41,760
Mrs.

234
00:07:41,940 --> 00:07:44,292
MacLeod kept her butter in a covered

235
00:07:44,292 --> 00:07:46,514
dish on the windowsill all winter.

236
00:07:46,694 --> 00:07:49,190
Ohio kitchens were cold enough.

237
00:07:49,540 --> 00:07:52,149
And if your shortbread does come out

238
00:07:52,149 --> 00:07:54,034
hard, don't throw it away.

239
00:07:54,214 --> 00:07:56,832
Crumble it over vanilla ice cream,

240
00:07:56,832 --> 00:07:59,604
or use it instead of graham crackers

241
00:07:59,604 --> 00:08:00,836
for a pie crust,

242
00:08:00,836 --> 00:08:03,455
or layer it with whipped cream and

243
00:08:03,455 --> 00:08:04,918
berries in a glass.

244
00:08:05,098 --> 00:08:06,143
Mrs.

245
00:08:06,323 --> 00:08:09,606
MacLeod said, there's no such thing as

246
00:08:09,606 --> 00:08:11,594
ruined shortbread, hen.

247
00:08:11,774 --> 00:08:14,430
Just shortbread that wants to be something

248
00:08:14,430 --> 00:08:14,746
else.

249
00:08:15,096 --> 00:08:16,758
Now, for Christmas,

250
00:08:16,758 --> 00:08:18,509
you can dress it up.

251
00:08:18,689 --> 00:08:20,760
Dip one end of each finger in

252
00:08:20,760 --> 00:08:23,188
melted chocolate and let it set on

253
00:08:23,188 --> 00:08:23,902
wax paper.

254
00:08:24,082 --> 00:08:26,477
Or mix in a handful of chopped

255
00:08:26,477 --> 00:08:29,592
dried cranberries and a spoon of orange

256
00:08:29,592 --> 00:08:29,991
zest.

257
00:08:30,171 --> 00:08:32,677
Or add a teaspoon of chopped fresh

258
00:08:32,677 --> 00:08:33,341
rosemary.

259
00:08:33,521 --> 00:08:35,029
That sounds strange,

260
00:08:35,029 --> 00:08:37,441
honey, but it's the one my grown

261
00:08:37,441 --> 00:08:39,929
grandchildren ask for every year.

262
00:08:40,279 --> 00:08:42,718
Or make little shortbread cutouts.

263
00:08:42,898 --> 00:08:45,865
Roll the chilled dough between two sheets

264
00:08:45,865 --> 00:08:48,398
of parchment, a quarter inch thick,

265
00:08:48,398 --> 00:08:49,846
cut stars and trees,

266
00:08:49,846 --> 00:08:52,524
and bake them for fifteen to eighteen

267
00:08:52,524 --> 00:08:53,103
minutes.

268
00:08:53,283 --> 00:08:55,633
They hold their shape perfectly,

269
00:08:55,633 --> 00:08:58,277
because there's no egg and no baking

270
00:08:58,277 --> 00:09:00,260
powder to make them spread.

271
00:09:00,440 --> 00:09:02,788
Shortbread is the best dough there is

272
00:09:02,788 --> 00:09:03,993
for cookie cutters.

273
00:09:04,342 --> 00:09:07,468
Shortbread keeps longer than any cookie I

274
00:09:07,468 --> 00:09:07,849
know.

275
00:09:08,029 --> 00:09:09,903
Two weeks in a tin at room

276
00:09:09,903 --> 00:09:13,219
temperature, and it actually gets better after

277
00:09:13,219 --> 00:09:14,012
a few days,

278
00:09:14,012 --> 00:09:16,318
as the butter flavor settles in.

279
00:09:16,498 --> 00:09:18,265
It mails beautifully,

280
00:09:18,265 --> 00:09:20,788
packed tight in a tin with wax

281
00:09:20,788 --> 00:09:21,293
paper.

282
00:09:21,473 --> 00:09:23,910
And it freezes for three months,

283
00:09:23,910 --> 00:09:25,281
baked or as dough.

284
00:09:25,461 --> 00:09:27,357
I bake mine the first week of

285
00:09:27,357 --> 00:09:29,710
December and forget about it until I

286
00:09:29,710 --> 00:09:30,233
need it.

287
00:09:30,582 --> 00:09:32,765
And do what Mrs.

288
00:09:32,945 --> 00:09:34,373
MacLeod did.

289
00:09:34,553 --> 00:09:36,933
A round of shortbread in a tin,

290
00:09:36,933 --> 00:09:38,698
tied with plaid ribbon,

291
00:09:38,698 --> 00:09:41,078
for every house on your street.

292
00:09:41,258 --> 00:09:43,940
It costs about two dollars a tin.

293
00:09:44,120 --> 00:09:47,137
When she passed in nineteen seventy-four,

294
00:09:47,137 --> 00:09:50,154
at ninety-one, every family on Elm Street

295
00:09:50,154 --> 00:09:51,626
came to the funeral,

296
00:09:51,626 --> 00:09:54,055
and the minister said she had fed

297
00:09:54,055 --> 00:09:56,483
the whole street for fifty years,

298
00:09:56,483 --> 00:09:57,808
one tin at a time.

299
00:09:58,158 --> 00:09:59,788
So one more time,

300
00:09:59,788 --> 00:10:00,363
honey.

301
00:10:00,543 --> 00:10:02,285
One, two, three.

302
00:10:02,465 --> 00:10:03,706
One part sugar,

303
00:10:03,706 --> 00:10:05,527
two parts good butter,

304
00:10:05,527 --> 00:10:07,016
three parts flour,

305
00:10:07,016 --> 00:10:10,244
and a little cornstarch for tenderness.

306
00:10:10,424 --> 00:10:12,653
Soft butter, not melted.

307
00:10:12,833 --> 00:10:14,029
Gentle hands.

308
00:10:14,209 --> 00:10:15,792
Prick it, score it,

309
00:10:15,792 --> 00:10:16,542
chill it.

310
00:10:16,722 --> 00:10:19,381
Three twenty-five, pale as sand.

311
00:10:19,561 --> 00:10:21,505
Cut it warm and cool it in

312
00:10:21,505 --> 00:10:22,103
the pan.

313
00:10:22,283 --> 00:10:23,572
That's Mrs.

314
00:10:23,752 --> 00:10:26,890
MacLeod's shortbread, and it has never once

315
00:10:26,890 --> 00:10:27,839
been a brick.

316
00:10:28,189 --> 00:10:29,980
Tell me in the comments,

317
00:10:29,980 --> 00:10:30,801
sweetheart.

318
00:10:30,981 --> 00:10:33,899
Did somebody on your street bring cookies

319
00:10:33,899 --> 00:10:34,824
at Christmas?

320
00:10:35,004 --> 00:10:36,954
I'd love to hear about them.

321
00:10:37,134 --> 00:10:40,297
The whole playlist of Grandma's recipes is

322
00:10:40,297 --> 00:10:42,330
right there on your screen,

323
00:10:42,330 --> 00:10:44,438
and I'll see you in the next

324
00:10:44,438 --> 00:10:44,890
video.

325
00:10:45,070 --> 00:10:46,683
Good night, hen.

326
00:10:46,863 --> 00:10:48,361
Merry Christmas.
